{"title":"YWANG Generalization","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>YWANG Generalization for Polynomial Factorization</b> (discovered by AoPS user <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/user/593414\">Yufanwang</a>) is a generalization of polynomial factorization. It differs from traditional techniques (such as <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division\" title=\"Synthetic division\">synthetic division</a>) in that the coefficients of each term is explicitly defined, and is applicable to the factoring of more than one element of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/1/7711bb834d82d95c067dae32fdf086b174f6e792.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x-a_i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> at the same time.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Theorem\">Theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=YWANG_Generalization&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>As per traditional techniques, we limit our constants to be within <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906fddead65545297c74bbc2ca83cd01f3f9ecc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}$\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" /> (i.e. we will define <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/8/4c8ff37e857e382b4c95ef25953d233db7209c60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_0, c_1, \\cdots, c_n \\in \\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"136\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/8/4b8f5e65021bc0186502c8f7e0479930929cc160.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i\\in \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/5/8f56ae7bb91350d810fe4d7a811fa79fb57059a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\in \\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" />). Then, for a given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/c/4ac26a4b7314c357652bce1c414b4cd16e621a60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)=c_0x^n+c_1x^{n-1}+\\cdots+c_{n-1}x+c_n=\\textstyle\\sum^n_{m=0}c_mx^{n-m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"457\" height=\"22\" /> and its factors <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/2/5c278eff24ef7067d506eaf1057862e764095915.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x-a_1)(x-a_2)\\cdots(x-a_i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"219\" height=\"20\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/f/e8f9c23e4e88aee09fd6961cde4114ca3da017c9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(x)=\\prod^i_{k=0}(x-a_k)\\sum^{n-1}_{m=0}\\gamma_{i, m}x^{n-m-1}\\]\" width=\"270\" height=\"51\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/1/2f1f0d28839be8cb5fed318a0ecee760796162d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma_{i,m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"33\" height=\"15\" /> designates the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />th term of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />th <a class=\"mw-selflink-fragment\" href=\"#YWANG_Gamma_Sequences\">YWANG gamma sequence</a>. Each term of the gamma sequence is defined as follows: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/9/6c92a11271c57a2d625dfa52397fae876fee616a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\gamma_{i,j}=\\sum_{k=0}^j\\left(c_k\\sum_{\\substack{b_1\\cdots b_i\\in [0,1]\\cap\\mathbb{Z} \\\\ \\sum_{l=1}^i b_l = k}} \\left(\\prod_{l=1}^i a_l^{b_l}\\right)\\right).\\]\" width=\"298\" height=\"96\" /> This result can be derived from strong division with <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division\" title=\"Synthetic division\">synthetic division</a> or by taking the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Taylor_polynomial\" title=\"Taylor polynomial\">Taylor series</a> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/2/9025ff5b3876b2e9d134789e584fccb9aa66a16a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\sum_{m=0}^n c_m x^{n-m}}{\\prod_{k=0}^i (x-a_k)}\\]\" width=\"113\" height=\"47\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/b/17b293a71101c683fe6f6468b44f49b6ca3ea494.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\infty$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"9\" />. A rigorous proof with both methods will be added in due time.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"YWANG_Gamma_Sequences\">YWANG Gamma Sequences</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=YWANG_Generalization&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: YWANG Gamma Sequences\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />th <b>YWANG gamma sequence</b> is given in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/9/6c92a11271c57a2d625dfa52397fae876fee616a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\gamma_{i,j}=\\sum_{k=0}^j\\left(c_k\\sum_{\\substack{b_1\\cdots b_i\\in [0,1]\\cap\\mathbb{Z} \\\\ \\sum_{l=1}^i b_l = k}} \\left(\\prod_{l=1}^i a_l^{b_l}\\right)\\right).\\]\" width=\"298\" height=\"96\" /> We will consider the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />th YWANG gamma sequence <b>solved</b> if there are not nested sums/products within the final expression. \n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solved_YWANG_Gamma_Sequences\">Solved YWANG Gamma Sequences</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=YWANG_Generalization&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solved YWANG Gamma Sequences\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>A list of currently solved YWANG gamma sequences follows.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/9/0a9a5f207bd147adbc8859d17a31e134731e5924.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\gamma_{1,j} &amp;= \\sum_{k=0}^j c_k a_1^{j-k} \\\\ \\gamma_{2,j} &amp;= \\sum_{k=0}^j c_k \\frac{a_1^{j-k}-a_2^{j-k}}{a_1-a_2} \\\\ \\gamma_{3,j} &amp;= \\sum_{k=0}^j c_k \\frac{a_1^{2(j-k)-1}(a_3-a_2)+a_2^{2(j-k)-1}(a_1-a_3)+a_3^{2(j-k)-1}(a_2-a_1)}{(a_1-a_2)(a_2-a_3)(a_3-a_1)} \\end{align*}\" width=\"559\" height=\"175\" />\nThis list will be updated as further gamma sequences are solved.\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017142916\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.013 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.023 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 104/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 31/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 3870/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:23776:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017142916 and revision id 248365. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}